.SS compiler
The GHC to build with.
\f[CR]name\f[R] selects one of the driver\(cqs own compilers;
\f[CR]package\f[R] supplies one from outside them, and the fields around
it are the attributes the drivers read off a compiler.
\f[CR]platforms\f[R] gives cross targets their own compiler and
toolchain; a platform without an entry uses the fields above it.
.PP
A compiler that has to be described this way is worth writing once: the
modules under \f[CR]nix\-haskell\-compilers\f[R] are ready\-made entries
for compilers distributed outside the drivers\(cq package sets.
.PP
\f[I]Type:\f[R] submodule
.PP
\f[I]Default:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{ }
.EE
.PP
\f[I]Example:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{
  name = \(dqghc912\(dq;

  \f[I]# a bindist for the wasm target, with the toolchain it was built\f[R]
  \f[I]# with, as \(ganix\-haskell\-compilers/ghc\-wasm\-meta\(ga supplies it\f[R]
  platforms.wasi32 = {
    package = wasm\-ghc;
    version = \(dq9.12.4.20260731\(dq;
    targetPrefix = \(dqwasm32\-wasi\-\(dq;
    enableShared = true;
    haskell\-nix.libDir = \(dqlib\(dq;
    nixpkgs.enableExternalInterpreter = false;
    toolchain = {
      package = wasi\-sdk;
      cc = \(dqwasm32\-wasi\-clang\(dq;
      ar = \(dqllvm\-ar\(dq;
      ld = \(dqwasm\-ld\(dq;
      strip = \(dqllvm\-strip\(dq;
    };
  };
}
.EE
.PP
