\f[I]Declared by:\f[R] \- \c
.UR file://%3Cnix-haskell%3E/modules/cross/wasm
/modules/cross/wasm
.UE \c
.SS js\-optimize
A linked \f[CR].jsexe\f[R] directory with its \f[CR]all.js\f[R]
closure\-compiled, the rest of the directory as it was.
It takes the directory rather than the package that carries it:
.IP
.EX
js\-optimize {
  platform = \(dqghcjs\(dq;
  package = \(dqfrontend\(dq;
  exe = \(dqfrontend\(dq;
  jsexe = \(dq${frontend}/bin/frontend.jsexe\(dq;
}
.EE
.PP
The three names are only what the settings are looked up under, and any
of them can be left out to say nothing about it.
\f[CR]closure\f[R] is read from the layer that states a field most
specifically to the least:
\f[CR]platforms.<platform>.packages.<package>.components.exes.<exe>.closure\f[R],
\f[CR]platforms.<platform>.packages.<package>.closure\f[R],
\f[CR]platforms.<platform>.closure\f[R], then the same package and
executable layers of \f[CR]packages\f[R], and last \f[CR]closure\f[R]
itself, which is the only one holding values throughout.
They are read from the project\(cqs own values rather than a
driver\(cqs, since this runs on a built artifact, outside any driver.
.PP
\f[I]Type:\f[R] function that evaluates to a(n) package
.PP
\f[I]Default:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
<nix\-haskell>/libs/closure.nix, run with the settings the named target,
package and executable resolve to
.EE
.PP
