.UE \c
.SS platforms
Per\-platform customization, keyed by \f[CR]pkgs.pkgsCross\f[R] platform
name (the keys of \f[CR]shell.crossPlatforms\f[R] and
\f[CR]projectCross\f[R]).
.PP
A cabal file or project file can make a package\(cqs flags, and through
them its dependencies, conditional on the platform.
The haskell.nix driver follows those conditionals through its solver;
the nixpkgs driver has none, so what they would have decided is given
here.
The flags reach the point where a package\(cqs dependencies are worked
out, rather than only how it is configured.
.PP
\f[CR]wasm\-opt\f[R] and \f[CR]closure\f[R] are the bundle optimizer
settings for whatever is built for this target, which the
\f[CR]packages\f[R] entries under them narrow to one package, and their
\f[CR]components.exes\f[R] entries to one executable of it.
.PP
\f[I]Type:\f[R] attribute set of (submodule)
.PP
\f[I]Default:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{ }
.EE
.PP
\f[I]Example:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{
  wasi32.wasm\-opt.level = \(dqz\(dq;
  wasi32.packages.reflex\-dom.flags.use\-warp = false;
}
.EE
.PP
\f[I]Declared by:\f[R] \- \c
.UR file://%3Cnix-haskell%3E/modules/common.nix
/modules/common.nix
.UE \c
.SS platforms.<name>.packages
Per\-package customization for this platform only, merged over the
project\-wide \f[CR]packages\f[R].
The fields are the same, with \f[CR]bundles\f[R] added: what a driver
built for this target, shipped.
.PP
\f[I]Type:\f[R] attribute set of (submodule)
.PP
\f[I]Default:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{ }
.EE
.PP
\f[I]Example:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{
  splitmix.patches = [ ./splitmix\-js.patch ];
  reflex\-dom\-core.doCheck = false;
  my\-app.flags.production = true;
}
.EE
